ao_coreaudio_exclusive: wait for the virtual format before using it

init() sets the physical format through ca_change_physical_format_sync(),
which waits only until the PHYSICAL format reports the change, then reads
the VIRTUAL format at once. On macOS 27 (2026-11-05, Jabra Link 380 USB
headset, mpv master 9028187538 and dc4b285c93 alike) the virtual format
follows later, so init sometimes snapshots the previous one (float instead
of s16). When the real update lands, property_listener_cb sees a virtual
format different from the snapshot and reloads. uninit then restores the
original mixable format, whose late notification lands on the next
instance's listener, and so on: the log alternates
"AO: [coreaudio_exclusive] ... float" / "... s16" with "Stream format
changed! Reloading." about twice a second, 10 opens in 7 s, until
"can't start audio device ([35]...)". Afterwards the device was unusable
for shared output until coreaudiod restarted.

Wait, bounded to 500 ms, until the virtual format agrees with the physical
one (equal for a non-mixable physical format, or no longer changing for a
mixable one) before taking the snapshot.

Not upstream yet. Drop this patch once mpv waits for the virtual format
itself.

--- a/audio/out/ao_coreaudio_exclusive.c
+++ b/audio/out/ao_coreaudio_exclusive.c
@@ -110,6 +110,48 @@
     return noErr;
 }
 
+// The virtual format follows a physical format change asynchronously, and on
+// macOS 27 (seen with a USB headset) it can still report the previous format
+// after ca_change_physical_format_sync() has returned. Snapshotting it then
+// makes the late update look like an outside change: property_listener_cb
+// reloads, the reload's restore of the original format echoes into the next
+// instance's listener, and the device is grabbed and released twice a second
+// until it fails to start. Wait, bounded, until the virtual format agrees.
+static void wait_virtual_format_settled(struct ao *ao, AudioStreamID stream)
+{
+    int64_t start = mp_time_ns();
+    int64_t deadline = start + MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(500);
+    AudioStreamBasicDescription last = {0};
+    bool have_last = false;
+    while (1) {
+        AudioStreamBasicDescription phys, virt;
+        if (CA_GET(stream, kAudioStreamPropertyPhysicalFormat, &phys) != noErr ||
+            CA_GET(stream, kAudioStreamPropertyVirtualFormat, &virt) != noErr)
+            return;
+        // A non-mixable physical format is used as is, so the virtual format
+        // must equal it; a mixable one is mixed in float, so only wait for the
+        // virtual format to stop changing.
+        bool settled = (phys.mFormatFlags & kAudioFormatFlagIsNonMixable)
+            ? ca_asbd_equals(&phys, &virt)
+            : (have_last && ca_asbd_equals(&last, &virt));
+        int64_t now = mp_time_ns();
+        if (settled) {
+            if (now - start >= MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(10)) {
+                MP_VERBOSE(ao, "virtual format settled after %.0f ms\n",
+                           MP_TIME_NS_TO_MS(now - start));
+            }
+            return;
+        }
+        if (now >= deadline) {
+            MP_WARN(ao, "virtual format did not settle within 500 ms\n");
+            return;
+        }
+        last = virt;
+        have_last = true;
+        mp_sleep_ns(MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(5));
+    }
+}
+
 static OSStatus enable_property_listener(struct ao *ao, bool enabled)
 {
     struct priv *p = ao->priv;
@@ -336,6 +378,7 @@
     // Even if changing the physical format fails, we can try using the current
     // virtual format.
     ca_change_physical_format_sync(ao, p->stream, hwfmt);
+    wait_virtual_format_settled(ao, p->stream);
 
     if (!ca_init_chmap(ao, p->device))
         goto coreaudio_error;
